At 950 nm, optical radiation sits in the Infrared-A (IR-A) band (780 nm to 1,400 nm)

Because 950 nm is invisible to the human eye, it presents unique risks: 
	the eye's natural aversion reflex (blinking or turning away) is NOT triggered. 
	As a result, the eye can be exposed to elevated levels of radiation without any immediate warning.  

Under BS EN 62471 / IEC 62471, light sources emitting at 950 nm (such as IR illumination LEDs, LiDAR, night-vision illuminators, 
or industrial sensors) are evaluated primarily against three main hazards:
	Retinal Thermal Hazard – Weak Visual Stimulus (Section 4.3.6)

The Mechanism: 
	Because the beam focuses onto a small area of the retina, high power densities can cause localized thermal burns on retinal tissue.

The Weighting Factor R(λ): 
	The standard applies a spectral weighting function R(λ) to adjust for how effectively different wavelengths damage the retina. At 950 nm:
	R(950 nm) = 10^(0.002⋅(700−950)) ≈ 0.316
Why it matters: 
	Even though R(λ) drops at 950 nm compared to visible light (R=1.0), the absence of a blink reflex means the exposure duration t used in calculations 
	is much longer (often default evaluated up to 10s or 1000s rather than the 0.25s aversion limit)

The notes for the SFH4545:
Notes
The evaluation of eye safety occurs according to the standard IEC 62471:2006 (photo biological safety of
lamps and lamp systems). Within the risk grouping system of this IEC standard, the device specified in this
data sheet falls into the class exempt group (exposure time 10000 s). Under real circumstances (for expo-
sure time, conditions of the eye pupils, observation distance), it is assumed that no endangerment to the
eye exists from these devices. As a matter of principle, however, it should be mentioned that intense light
sources have a high secondary exposure potential due to their blinding effect. When looking at bright light
sources (e.g. headlights), temporary reduction in visual acuity and afterimages can occur, leading to irrita-
tion, annoyance, visual impairment, and even accidents, depending on the situation.

If a different emitter is used, it may no longer be safe!
